<section class="hero">

  <div>
    <span class="eyebrow"><b>●</b> Behaviour in the markup</span>
    <h1>PAD writes the page.<br><span class="grad">Alpine wakes it up.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Alpine adds behaviour to HTML with a few attributes - and PAD writes that HTML. Both
      like braces, so the trick is to let each have its own: PAD hands Alpine its state as JSON
      with the ^ sigil, and longer components live in a file of <code>www/</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn" href="?examples/state">Start with state from PHP</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="?guide">Braces, data and requests</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="hero-code">
{highlight 'pad'}
<div x-data="{^profile}">          <!-- $profile from the page's PHP -->
  <h2 x-text="name"></h2>
  <button @click="likes++">
    ♥ <span x-text="likes"></span>
  </button>
</div>
{/highlight}
    <p class="small muted">The template holds no brace of Alpine's: the state is the JSON of a PHP array.</p>
  </div>

</section>

{groups}
<div class="group-head">
  <h2>{$title}</h2>
  <span class="badge">{$count} examples</span>
</div>
<p class="group-text">{$text}</p>
<div class="grid">
  {examples}
  <a class="card" href="?{$page}">
    <span class="card-icon" aria-hidden="true">{$icon}</span>
    <span class="card-go" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <h3>{$title}</h3>
    <p>{$lede}</p>
    <div class="chips">{server}<code class="chip is-server">{$server}</code>{/server}{client}<code class="chip is-client">{$client}</code>{/client}</div>
  </a>
  {/examples}
</div>
{/groups}
